WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.360
Close your eyes and think of the worst&nbsp;
movie you saw in 2021. Got it? Good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.360
Now forget it — because we can pretty much&nbsp;
guarantee that these were a whole lot worse.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.960
From The Exorcist to The Devils to The Omen,&nbsp;
the 1970s were undoubtedly a golden age for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:18.960
religious horror movies. There have been a&nbsp;
few standout additions to the subgenre since,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:22.560
but most contemporary faith-based horrors&nbsp;
fail to capture the haunting magic of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.520
aforementioned classics — and 2021's The&nbsp;
Unholy is a prime example. It's the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:31.120
of a hearing-impaired girl named Alice,&nbsp;
who, after a visit from the Virgin Mary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:35.040
finds that she has gained the ability to&nbsp;
heal the sick. What could possibly go wrong?

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:44.320
"I slit their windpipes and watched&nbsp;
the breath slowly leak out."

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:49.600
As it turns out: a lot. To put a long story short,&nbsp;
The Unholy is a tale of witchcraft, deception,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:54.400
creepy dolls, and, uh, journalism. Disgraced&nbsp;
reporter Gerry Fenn accidentally released&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.320
the spirit of a witch who then possessed&nbsp;
Alice under the guise of the Virgin Mary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.560
Naturally, it's up to him to save her from the&nbsp;
clutches of the aforementioned grumpy crone.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.800
Cricket Brown and Jeffrey Dean Morgan do their&nbsp;
utmost to elevate a cliche-ridden script,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.960
but the by-the-numbers plot, predictable jump&nbsp;
scares, and substandard special effects make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.360
The Unholy a genuinely unenjoyable&nbsp;
experience. Perhaps unsurprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.280
the majority of critics had nothing but bad&nbsp;
things to say about this unholy misfire.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.080
Based on the real-life crimes of the Truck Stop&nbsp;
Killer, Midnight in the Switchgrass follows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:28.560
two FBI agents, Karl Helter and his partner&nbsp;
Rebecca Lombardi, who are hunting a serial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:32.400
killer at work in Florida's underworld. When&nbsp;
Lombardi is kidnapped by the man they're after,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.920
Helter teams up with a state-level cop&nbsp;
to get her back and catch the killer.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:39.600
Despite featuring prominently in the&nbsp;
promotional material, Bruce Willis appears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:44.080
in precious few scenes in this movie — as is&nbsp;
pretty much the norm with his films nowadays.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:47.360
Modern Willis movies also tend to get&nbsp;
a real hammering from the critics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:51.520
and Midnight in the Switchgrass is no&nbsp;
exception. As The New York Times' review said:

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:55.760
"The atmosphere is thoroughly sleazy without being&nbsp;
distinctive, and everything about the movie — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:00.240
emotionless line readings, the half-baked&nbsp;
backstories — exudes a terse functionality."

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:04.000
When the dust had settled, this thoroughly&nbsp;
lazy thriller was left with a damning score&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.640
of just 8% from critics on Rotten Tomatoes.&nbsp;
The only people who might gain something from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:12.640
Midnight in the Switchgrass are fans of Megan&nbsp;
Fox and Machine Gun Kelly. Their relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.120
began on the set of the movie, but even they're&nbsp;
under no illusions about its quality. The couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:21.840
pulled out of the premiere, and Kelly appeared&nbsp;
to call the film "trash" in a tweet days later.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:26.240
By all rights, Naked Singularity should've&nbsp;
been one of the best movies of 2021.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:30.240
Produced by Ridley Scott and featuring&nbsp;
the likes of Olivia Cooke and John Boyega,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:34.960
this unconventional comedy-drama had all the&nbsp;
makings of a modern classic. Boyega stars as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:39.200
New York City public defender going through a&nbsp;
moral crisis. After learning that a client is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:44.000
involved in a $50 million drug deal, he decides&nbsp;
to steal the money from right under their nose.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.720
There are plenty of good ideas here, but&nbsp;
sloppy execution lets Naked Singularity down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:53.280
Threads that were fully fleshed out in Sergio&nbsp;
De La Pava's original novel go nowhere here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.920
and writer-director Chase Palmer doesn't seem&nbsp;
to know what genre his adaptation belongs to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.920 --> 00:02:59.520
As The New York Times puts it:

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:04.160
"Naked Singularity welds legal drama and&nbsp;
science fiction into a misshapen crime caper."

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:10.320
When a film has a 0% rating on Rotten Tomatoes&nbsp;
a month on from its release, it's usually safe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:14.400
to say that it's not going to be remembered as&nbsp;
a classic. And Habit, the debut feature from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:19.280
writer-director Janell Shirtcliff, probably won't&nbsp;
be remembered by anyone but diehard fans of Bella&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:24.320
Thorne. The former Disney Channel actor stars&nbsp;
as a Los Angeles socialite who pays the bills by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:29.360
selling drugs for a washed-up Hollywood star. When&nbsp;
a drug deal goes sour and some cash is stolen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.920
she and her hard-partying pals go on the&nbsp;
run. Dressed as nuns, for some reason.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:38.160
It really does seem like Shirtcliff woke up one&nbsp;
day and decided that a bunch of L.A. girls beating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:42.320
the hell out of a guy while dressed as nuns would&nbsp;
look cool on camera — and then proceeded to build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:46.960
an entire movie around that idea. There was&nbsp;
some backlash to the film when it was announced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:51.440
mostly to do with the casting of Paris Jackson&nbsp;
as Jesus. But the final product is more of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:56.400
crime against cinema than anything else. Habit&nbsp;
tries so very hard to be edgy; so much so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:00.800
the movie's press release actually described&nbsp;
it as being "reminiscent of early Tarantino."

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:08.480
"The truth is you're the weak.&nbsp;
And I'm the tyranny of evil men."

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:14.160
But the plot is paper-thin, and there's next to&nbsp;
nothing in terms of character development. Variety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:18.480
deftly summed up this mercifully short film with&nbsp;
a single sentence in its review, calling it:

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:21.520
"[A] candy-colored dress-up excuse&nbsp;
for a bunch of slumming models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:23.840
rock musicians, and miscellaneous scenesters."

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:29.120
Where did it all go wrong for Neill Blomkamp?&nbsp;
At one stage, the South African director was in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.960
line to take charge of a new Alien movie,&nbsp;
but his stock has fallen significantly in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:36.640
recent years. In an interview with The&nbsp;
Guardian, Blompkamp recently joked:

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:39.280
"It's possible that Ridley [Scott]&nbsp;
watched Chappie, and he was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:42.000
this guy can't do Alien so let's&nbsp;
just go ahead and move on."

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.720
He was speaking to the British newspaper to&nbsp;
promote his 2021 supernatural horror flick Demonic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:52.480
which, unfortunately for Blomkamp, got panned even&nbsp;
worse than his much-derided 2015 sci-fi flick.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:56.000
Chappie and Demonic are beset by&nbsp;
similar issues — they're just a lot more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.240
apparent in the latter. Filmed during the&nbsp;
COVID-19 pandemic, Demonic is a far more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:04.480
contained movie and therefore can't always&nbsp;
hide behind its VFX. It follows a young woman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:09.120
who gets the chance to go inside the mind of her&nbsp;
comatose serial killer mother via new technology.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.040
When she enters the simulation, however, she&nbsp;
finds a demon, and it turns out that her mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:17.360
was possessed, and that the whole experiment&nbsp;
is being overseen by a Vatican black-ops unit.

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:20.640
If this all sounds a little out&nbsp;
there, that's because it is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:25.200
What's worse is that the director seems&nbsp;
unwilling to fully commit to the bonkers premise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.400
As Brian Tallerico of RogerEbert.com put it:

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:32.560
"Demonic feels like a film made by someone who's&nbsp;
scared of his own ideas and his own limitations."

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.680
People who saw Romeo Must Die back in 2000&nbsp;
probably thought they'd never see the day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:43.200
that an even worse contemporary retelling of Romeo&nbsp;
and Juliet could hit cineplexes, but on July 16th,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:48.480
2021, that's exactly what happened. Die in&nbsp;
a Gunfight stars Diego Boneta and Alexandra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:54.160
Daddario as Romeo and Juliet — or, actually, Ben&nbsp;
and Mary, two star-crossed lovers from powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:58.880
families that have been at war since the 1860s.&nbsp;
Their families are determined to keep them apart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:02.560
but as you've almost certainly figured out by&nbsp;
now, they disregard the long-running feud and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:06.560
follow their hearts instead. Vikings&nbsp;
star Travis Fimmel also shows up as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:10.960
typically intense hitman, and the film is&nbsp;
narrated by Billy Crudup for some reason.

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:15.440
Upon seeing it, it becomes clear why this&nbsp;
movie spent over a decade in development hell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:19.760
Zac Efron was all set to star in and&nbsp;
produce Die in a Gunfight in 2010.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:24.720
Variety later claimed that Josh Hutcherson and&nbsp;
Kaya Scodelario had agreed to play Ben and Mary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:28.160
but that also fell through. Would any&nbsp;
of these actors have been able to save&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:32.960
the movie? Boneta and Daddario don't exactly&nbsp;
shine, though their characters are so thinly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:36.640
written that it's little wonder so many&nbsp;
actors passed on the project beforehand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:41.200
They certainly weren't helped by director Collin&nbsp;
Schiffli, who opts for action movie clichés and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:45.840
excessive neon lighting over any meaningful&nbsp;
exploration of the protagonists' characters.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:50.400
If you've already forgotten about the&nbsp;
2019 animated feature The Addams Family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:54.640
you're not alone. Despite boasting an&nbsp;
impressive voice cast, including Oscar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.640 --> 00:07:00.000
Isaac and Charlize Theron as Gomez and Morticia,&nbsp;
the kid-friendly reboot of the classic property&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.840
wasn't exactly a hit with the critics —&nbsp;
though low production costs meant it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:08.080
still able to turn a healthy profit. A second&nbsp;
film was announced pretty much immediately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:12.000
and the A-list voice cast all signed on&nbsp;
to reprise their roles. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:16.400
their combined talents were not enough to save&nbsp;
this flat and equally forgettable follow-up.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:19.600
The Addams Family 2 is the kind of&nbsp;
film that could only be enjoyed by an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:24.720
Addams — because it's torture. This painfully&nbsp;
unfunny sequel follows the titular family on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:30.240
campervan trip organized by Gomez, who fears the&nbsp;
Addams clan isn't as tight-knit as it once was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:34.800
Detective Pikachu scribes Dan Hernandez and&nbsp;
Benji Samit were among the numerous writers who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:39.120
contributed to the script, which is nevertheless&nbsp;
paper thin, extremely light on laughs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:44.880
and nowhere near kooky enough for longtime Addams&nbsp;
Family fans. Empire Magazine said of the film:

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:49.120
"Ironically, given the mantra for its main&nbsp;
characters is about embracing the weird, The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:54.320
Addams Family 2 does little that is out-there or&nbsp;
different, delivering a safe, stale 93 minutes."

00:07:55.280 --> 00:08:00.000
The Halloween franchise got a fresh start in&nbsp;
2018 when a brand new installment blew up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.240
box office and scored rave reviews. This&nbsp;
new Halloween ignored all the films that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:09.040
had followed 1978's Halloween and continued&nbsp;
the story of Laurie Strode decades later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:13.760
The critics loved it, too... though the same&nbsp;
cannot be said of 2021's Halloween Kills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:17.360
the shockingly bland second entry&nbsp;
in a planned sequel trilogy.

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:21.120
Halloween Kills begins immediately after the&nbsp;
conclusion of the previous film, in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:25.440
Laurie locked escaped killer Michael Myers in&nbsp;
the basement of her house and set it ablaze.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:29.920
It soon becomes clear that responding firefighters&nbsp;
released him, and he's coming for Laurie once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:34.320
again. But here's what seemed to annoy people:&nbsp;
Towards the end of the movie, Myers is set upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:40.320
by an angry mob, taking a beating that no mortal&nbsp;
man could survive. Yet he does. After being shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:45.360
and stabbed multiple times, Michael gets up and&nbsp;
proceeds to slaughter the now-terrified townsfolk.

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:48.560
This ending caused confusion and&nbsp;
disappointment in equal measure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:52.240
seemingly confirming that Michael Myers&nbsp;
draws power from supernatural sources.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:56.320
Previous Halloween sequels have taken this&nbsp;
path, and the original did actually hint at it.

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:00.400
"I killed him."
"But you can't kill the boogeyman."

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:07.760
But nobody expected this movie to&nbsp;
follow suit. Making Michael truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:11.600
immortal was a major cop-out that only&nbsp;
served to set up the final installment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:14.800
As such, Halloween Kills is a&nbsp;
pointless stepping stone of a movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:19.600
doing nothing to advance the overarching story —&nbsp;
and failing to deliver any real scares, either.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:25.440
Sometimes, something that works perfectly on&nbsp;
stage just doesn't make the same impact on screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:29.120
This is certainly the case with Dear Evan&nbsp;
Hansen, the story of a high school loner who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:33.840
gets swept up in a lie after a classmate dies&nbsp;
by suicide. On the advice of his therapist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:38.560
Evan writes letters to himself, some of which&nbsp;
mention his crush, Zoe. Connor, Zoe's brother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:42.880
snatches one of his letters before taking his own&nbsp;
life, which leads everyone to assume the letter is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:48.160
Connor's last missive to Evan. Instead of coming&nbsp;
clean, Evan manufactures a fake friendship between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:52.720
him and Connor, putting his parents' minds at&nbsp;
ease and allowing him to get closer to Zoe.

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:56.560
It's not easy to get behind a character who&nbsp;
would do such a thing, though Ben Platt made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.560 --> 00:10:01.040
it possible on Broadway. He reprises the&nbsp;
title role in the 2021 film adaptation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:05.200
which keeps the tunes but lacks the offbeat&nbsp;
charm. Without an audience to feed off of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:09.280
Platt's performance falls flat. As far as&nbsp;
the music is concerned, the songs don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:14.080
have the same impact as they did on stage.&nbsp;
As the Chicago-Sun Times review pointed out:

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:18.320
"Upbeat tunes and a heavy storyline&nbsp;
about teen suicide make a cringey combo."

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:22.000
If you or anyone you know&nbsp;
is having suicidal thoughts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:29.472
please call the National Suicide Prevention&nbsp;
Lifeline at 1-800-273-TALK (8255).

